Subject: [PATCH 09888/11362] Two QA-layer guard fixes: ledger reads every
 audit; mutation proofs are exclusive

FIX 1 -- the audit findings ledger ingested exactly one audit, and its own
origin was a different one.

scripts/audit_findings_ledger.py was hardcoded to ops/experiment_claim_audit_report.md.
Its docstring says it exists because "an audit whose findings accumulate unread is the
next silent-but-trusted layer -- the same shape as the QA-layer audit that wrote no
report for four weeks while its caller reported success." It was built because the
QA-layer audit failed silently, and it did not ingest the QA-layer audit. Its scope was
one audit while its stated concept was "flagged audit verdicts someone must answer":
a pattern narrower than its concept, in the guard written to fix that class.

Measured: milestone closes .572 and .573 produced 7 SILENT_NON_FIRING verdicts between
them. Zero were ingested. All 7 were hand-entered by the outer loop. The report is
REGENERATED at every close, so an un-ingested finding is not merely unread -- it is
overwritten by the next run.

Built a SOURCES registry. Each source's flagged set is IMPORTED from that audit's own
module, never copied. verifier_authenticity_audit.py and artifact_convention_audit.py
stay out, with the reason in the module: neither exposes a module-level constant, so
including them would mean the copy this module refuses on principle. The actual blocker
was _load_module not registering in sys.modules, which made @dataclass in the QA-layer
audit raise at class creation and its constant unreachable.

FIX 2 -- a hand-run mutation proof had no interlock.

The brief's premise was wrong and saying so changed the design. scripts/test_suite_
mutation_check.py is a DETECTOR with no mutation-application mode; nothing in this repo
writes MUTATED markers, and its module docstring already REJECTS a lock for the
--snapshot/<work>/--check flow, with a reason that still holds. So the interlock ships
as an additive proof SESSION -- --mutation-begin / --mutation-end /
--mutation-force-unlock -- touching none of the existing modes.

It carries an exclusive lock under --git-common-dir (shared by all ~10 worktrees), a
close-time scan that refuses while any MUTATED marker survives or the target is not
byte-identical, and an inert-run check that refuses when the file being mutated is not
the file the interpreter imports. That last one is the trap that nearly voided a real
proof set: __editable__.carnot_ebm-0.1.0b1.pth pins the MAIN checkout's absolute path,
so a worktree proof is silently inert -- every mutation reads GREEN while measuring
nothing.

THREE DEFECTS FOUND AFTER THE FIRST BUILD, each now pinned by a regression test.
Recorded rather than quietly patched:

1. Reclaim keyed on holder liveness LOCKED NOTHING. A session spans two CLI calls, so
   the process that wrote the lock has always exited; the rule reclaimed every time and
   printed "session OPEN" to a second caller. Age decides now; liveness may only ADD a
   refusal.
2. Scanning every changed file BRICKED the tool against this project's own
   documentation -- the spec and changelog quote the marker verbatim while describing
   the incident, so every open refused. A marker only does damage in a file that RUNS.
3. An unpinned --mutation-end skipped the ownership check entirely, so a second agent in
   a fresh shell closed whoever's session was open. Landing between two mutations the
   tree looked clean, so it succeeded -- the incident, rebuilt.

An independent adversarial reviewer returned 16 findings; 11 were acted on. Two were
already causing live damage: the widened ledger had appended 4 duplicate rows to
ops/audit-findings-ledger.md (two spellings of one finding, scripts/x.py vs x.py), and
the marker scan had bricked --mutation-begin on this checkout. Also fixed: untracked
directories collapsed by --porcelain, markers swept into a mid-session commit,
unscannable files treated as clean, a per-checkout lock in a multi-worktree repo,
_pid_alive's documented None-refusal never implemented, --gate unaware of an open
session, and find_spec importing 90 carnot modules inside a test.

The 4 duplicate rows are dispositioned WONTFIX in place with a note pointing at the
hand-entered row that still carries the finding as OPEN. Rows are never removed
(never-prune); this stops one finding escalating twice.

audit_findings_ledger.py added to qa_layer_authenticity_audit.py GUARD_TARGETS with a
written reason. Unit count 181 -> 182, so units_signature mismatches and rotation resets
to 0 -- the new target is audited first.

Spec: REQ-OPS-AUDIT-LEDGER-2, REQ-OPS-MUTATION-PROOF-1, 10 SCENARIOs.
Tests 55 -> 95. All 18 mutations RED on removal and GREEN on byte-identical restore, run
in a git worktree with the loaded module verified by import before any reading was
trusted. Four of the eighteen were decorative on first run and are now under test.

Not done, deliberately: the derived state-path sweep still misses str paths and paths
nested in containers, and the blast radius still misses a gitignored path. Both real,
neither on the incident path; recorded in ops/status.md.

